 August poll showing only a 4-point margin in a district that leans slightly Republican tempers the outlook, so the forecast sits at the lower end of Likely D.", "watch": "New polling or outside spending on the data-center issue could tighten the race if Conroy closes the gap in the final weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Greg Landsman (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Greg Landsman defends a Cincinnati-area district that leans slightly Republican after redistricting, against Republican Eric Conroy. Landsman holds a decisive financial advantage, with $3.6 million on hand compared to Conroy's $474,000, and has drawn little outside opposition spending so far. Ten expert ratings uniformly favor Landsman, mostly at Likely or Lean D, and prediction markets price his win chances near 86 to 87 percent.\n\nThe evidence is thinner on polling: a single August survey of likely voters showed Landsman ahead by just four points, and the race has centered on data-center development as a local issue. With a modestly favorable environment for Democrats and incumbency benefits, Landsman is favored, but the narrow poll and the district's Republican lean keep this short of safe territory.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8833, "margin": 7.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8451, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8473, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8639, "margin": 6.8}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8273, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7978, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.84, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.87, "margin": 6.5}]}